In short: We had a tested TMDS encoder, a revised serializer implementation, and a toolchain that could not complete differential output placement. We never reached the point of proving HDMI timing closure or displaying an HDMI image on the monitor.

That makes the HDMI implementation unfinished rather than demonstrated incorrect.

# My FPGA Toolchain Had Been Dead for Weeks and I Didn't Know

*4 September 2026 — The first physical video output from the SPU project.*

The first thing I managed to display on a monitor was eight VGA colour bars and a moving marker line.

Nothing particularly remarkable. Just a 640×480 image generated by an FPGA.

Except that getting those pixels onto the screen required discovering that my FPGA toolchain had been broken for weeks, that the HDMI output I'd implemented couldn't be placed by the open-source toolchain, and that the available documentation for the board's expansion header was inconsistent with the physical wiring.

I also lost a programmer along the way.

This is the story of those failures, how I isolated them, and what finally worked.

## 1. The FPGA builds weren't building

I'd spent weeks working on the RTL, trying to make progress with the graphics hardware. Builds were failing, and naturally I suspected something in the design.

Eventually I inspected the build directory.

There were no bitstreams.

Not outdated bitstreams. None at all.

The next step should have been one of the first:

```bash
ldd ~/.local/openxc7/bin/nextpnr-xilinx | grep 'not found'
```

Seven missing Boost shared libraries. Two more for `bbasm`.

The openXC7 binaries were linked against Boost 1.91, but my Arch Linux system had moved to Boost 1.92. The ABI had changed, and the binaries could no longer load their dependencies.

Every place-and-route invocation had been failing before it could even begin.

I briefly considered the usual workaround of symlinking the newer shared libraries under the older names. That didn't work. `bbasm` failed with an undefined symbol:

```text
_ZN5boost15program_options3argB5cxx11E
```

Fortunately, the older Boost package was still in my local package cache.

I extracted the required Boost 1.91 shared libraries from `boost-libs-1.91.0-2` and configured `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` to use them for the FPGA tools.

No system downgrade, no root access, and no need to rebuild the entire toolchain.

That solved the dependency problem.

The important lesson was not really about Boost.

**A broken FPGA toolchain and a broken FPGA design can produce exactly the same visible symptom: no bitstream.**

Before spending hours investigating RTL, verify that the synthesis, placement, routing, and bitstream tools can actually execute.

On a rolling-release Linux distribution, this is especially worth remembering. Keeping compatible tool dependencies isolated is considerably safer than assuming prebuilt binaries will survive every system update.

## 2. HDMI worked in simulation, but not in placement

The next obstacle was HDMI.

The project already contained an HDMI module, `hal_hdmi.v`, but investigation revealed that it had never actually been instantiated by any board's top-level design.

Its Xilinx implementation also serialized data in fabric at 250 MHz, which was not a realistic timing target for this configuration on the Artix-7 speed-grade −1 device.

I replaced that path with an implementation using the FPGA's OSERDESE2 primitives, operating at 125 MHz DDR.

The revised design synthesized successfully.

Then nextpnr-xilinx stopped progressing during placement.

Initially, there were several possible explanations: clocking, serializer constraints, primitive compatibility, placement legality, or a problem in the design.

Rather than continuing to modify the entire renderer, I reduced the test case and introduced one feature at a time.

| Test configuration | Result |
|---|---|
| No MMCM, no differential I/O | Builds and loads |
| Video logic with single-ended output | Pass |
| MMCM and video logic, single-ended | Pass |
| MMCM with OBUFDS/TMDS_33, no OSERDESE2 | Placement hangs |
| Complete HDMI path | Placement hangs |

This isolated the failure to differential output placement.

The problem was associated with an existing openXC7/nextpnr-xilinx limitation, tracked as issue #66, concerning differential outputs from IOSERDES blocks.

In the tested configuration, the MMCM wasn't the blocker. Neither was the serializer by itself.

The differential output path was.

I also lost time experimenting with the simulated-annealing placer, which reported:

```text
post-placement validity check failed for Bel .../A5FF (no cell)
```

That led me into investigating control sets and clocking before I established that this particular failure came from the placement flow rather than a demonstrated RTL defect.

The heap placer became the more useful baseline for these experiments.

### At least the HDMI encoder was tested

One useful result came out of the investigation.

The TMDS encoder had never had a dedicated testbench. I wrote one using an independent reference model derived from the DVI 1.0 specification.

The resulting verification suite passed **4,518 checks**:

- 4 control-symbol checks
- 512 data-symbol checks across both modes
- 4,000 running-disparity checks
- 2 negative-control checks

I also performed three mutation tests, deliberately introducing errors in the control word, DC-balance calculation, and `xnor_mode` threshold.

All three mutations were detected.

This provides substantial functional evidence for the encoder, although it is not an exhaustive correctness proof.

The HDMI output still couldn't be implemented through that placement flow, but the investigation had separated the encoder's functional behaviour from the physical implementation problem.

That distinction was useful.

**A successful simulation doesn't guarantee that a design can be placed and routed, and a placement failure doesn't necessarily mean the logic is wrong.**

## 3. Switching to VGA

Rather than let HDMI placement block the entire graphics project, I switched to VGA.

The objective was simple: get a stable, observable signal onto a physical monitor.

The board provided a 50 MHz oscillator. Dividing it by two gave a 25 MHz pixel clock suitable for the chosen 640×480 VGA timing.

For colour, I used one digital output per channel through a three-resistor DAC arrangement.

That gave eight possible colours.

The initial VGA test-pattern design used just **246 LUTs and 54 flip-flops**. Those figures describe the VGA colour-bar generator, not the later triangle rasterizer or GPU.

The output consisted of eight vertical colour bars and a horizontal marker line that moved down the screen.

Simple enough to debug with a logic analyser.

Or so I thought.

## 4. The monitor was on, but there was no picture

I initially made two mistakes.

The first was assuming that the monitor's green power LED meant it had locked onto a valid synchronization signal.

It didn't.

It simply indicated that the monitor was awake.

That small misunderstanding sent the debugging effort in the wrong direction.

The second mistake was trusting the board's expansion-header documentation without measuring the actual signals.

The QMTech README and LiteX platform file specified different pin orders for the J10 expansion header. Neither made the physical orientation sufficiently clear, and neither mapping was entirely correct for my setup.

I generated three bitstreams using inferred pin mappings.

None solved the problem.

Eventually I stopped guessing and built a diagnostic bitstream.

### The pin beacon

The diagnostic design drove eight header pins at different frequencies, each separated from the next by a factor of two.

That meant I could probe a pin with the logic analyser and recognize it from its frequency.

Instead of trying to infer which FPGA pin reached which VGA connector pin, the hardware could identify itself.

Two critical measurements were:

```text
VGA pin 13 — HSYNC — FPGA D5 — 6103.0 Hz
VGA pin 14 — VSYNC — FPGA E5 —  381.5 Hz
```

Both matched their assigned beacon frequencies.

The resulting mapping established that the J10 top row followed the LiteX order, while the bottom row did not.

Both previous constraint files had exactly one synchronization signal correctly assigned.

The beacon also proved useful for identifying unreliable measurements. On several occasions, a probe lost contact without an obvious warning.

A missing waveform could mean an inactive signal, an incorrect pin, or simply a disconnected probe.

**Before interpreting silence as a hardware failure, establish that the probe can detect a known-toggling signal at the same point.**

The entire pin-mapping problem became much easier once I had a measurement method that did not depend on the documentation being correct.

## 5. Finally, pixels on the monitor

With the corrected pin assignments, the VGA signal appeared.

I captured the physical synchronization signals using a logic analyser over two-second measurement windows.

| Measurement | Observed | Expected |
|---|---:|---:|
| HSYNC frequency | 31,248.25 Hz | 31,250 Hz |
| HSYNC duty cycle | 88.01% | 88.00% |
| Implied pixel clock | 24.9986 MHz | 25.0000 MHz |
| Implied frame rate | 59.520 Hz | 59.524 Hz |

The horizontal synchronization frequency was within approximately **0.006%** of the expected value.

More importantly, the sync duty cycle matched the expected 96 low cycles out of 800 pixel-clock cycles.

The colour channels also showed duty cycles of approximately 36.4%, close to the predicted 36.6%, providing an additional check that active-video and blanking intervals were behaving as expected.

And there they were: eight vertical colour bars, in order.

White, yellow, cyan, green, magenta, red, blue, and black.

The marker line moved vertically through the image.

The movement was important. Static colour bars could establish that a display was showing an image, but the moving marker demonstrated continued operation of the display pipeline.

This was the project's first physical video output on any board.

It wasn't a GPU yet. There was no triangle rasterization, depth buffer, shading, or dynamic scene submission.

But the essential display path was working.

The later rasterization experiments finally had a physical output system they could build upon.

## 6. The bench bit back

There was one more unpleasant surprise.

During the experiments, the FPGA programmer stopped functioning after the VGA harness was connected to the monitor.

It had previously worked for hours.

Then came a BOOTSEL reset, three USB bulk-write failures, and eventually a complete loss of USB enumeration.

Even BOOTSEL stopped appearing.

Because BOOTSEL is provided by mask ROM, its disappearance was especially concerning.

The leading explanation was an electrical ground-loop problem involving the mains-powered monitor, VGA ground, the FPGA board, the JTAG programmer, and USB ground.

A possible return path was:

```text
Monitor mains earth
        │
     VGA ground
        │
     FPGA ground
        │
      JTAG lead
        │
     Programmer
        │
      USB ground
        │
      Host earth
```

There was supporting circumstantial evidence.

With the monitor disconnected, a replacement programmer repeatedly completed full 3.8 MB writes without failure. Similar operations had failed four times with the monitor attached.

But **the ground-loop explanation remains a hypothesis, not a proven diagnosis**.

There was no direct fault-current measurement establishing the mechanism, and the programmer's failure alone cannot identify its cause.

An earlier modification had added series resistors to TCK, TMS, and TDI, but those would not prevent current flowing through the ground connections.

The incident reinforced an important lesson: bench wiring, shared grounds, and external equipment deserve the same attention as RTL and pin constraints.

## 7. What I took away

The first display milestone was modest: a moving test pattern on a VGA monitor.

But getting there exposed several distinct failure modes that initially looked like one problem.

The toolchain could not load its dependencies.

The HDMI implementation encountered an unsupported placement path.

An alternative placer produced a misleading diagnostic.

The physical expansion-header wiring didn't agree with the assumptions I'd made from the available documentation.

The monitor's LED told me less than I thought.

And the programmer failure introduced another problem whose exact electrical cause remains unresolved.

Each of these needed a different kind of investigation.

The techniques that finally helped were straightforward: inspecting tool dependencies, reducing failing designs to minimal test cases, using independent reference models, generating identifiable test signals, and measuring the physical hardware rather than trusting assumptions.

### What came next

On 5 September, the VGA display path was used to demonstrate a single rasterized triangle.

On 6 September, the design progressed to two overlapping triangles with per-pixel depth selection. That implementation matched an independent full-frame simulation oracle across all 307,200 pixels, while the physical display result was documented with monitor photographs.

Those were separate milestones, beyond the colour-bar demonstration described here.

The broader SPU project is investigating deterministic algebraic arithmetic, geometric computing, and an experimental CPU/GPU architecture. The first VGA output didn't validate those larger ideas.

It established something more basic, but necessary: a working physical display path and a reproducible foundation for subsequent graphics experiments.

The failed builds, incorrect pin constraints, measurements, testbenches, and implementation history are preserved in the repository.

I'd rather document the failures alongside the results. They're part of the engineering, and sometimes they're the most useful part for the next person trying to build something similar.

